// Day 5 question pool: High availability + scaling. Data only; ids key saved stats, never rename or reuse.
window.QUIZ.push(
{id:"q05-001", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Load balancing"],
 q:"An e-commerce company runs its cart, search, and checkout services on three separate fleets of EC2 instances. All three must be reachable over HTTPS through one public hostname, using the URL paths /cart, /search, and /checkout, and each fleet must scale independently. Which solution meets these requirements with the least operational overhead?",
 o:["Create a Network Load Balancer with a TLS listener for each service, each forwarding to that service's target group.",
    "Create three Application Load Balancers and use Route 53 weighted records to share one hostname between them.",
    "Create one Application Load Balancer whose HTTPS listener uses path-based rules, with one target group per service.",
    "Create a Gateway Load Balancer that inspects the URL path of each request and forwards it to the matching fleet."],
 a:[2],
 x:["An NLB works at layer 4 and cannot read URL paths; separate listeners would need separate ports, not one hostname on 443 split by path.",
    "Weighted records split traffic randomly by DNS weight, not by URL path, so requests would reach the wrong service; three ALBs also add cost and management.",
    "An ALB is a layer 7 load balancer: listener rules match the path and forward to a separate target group for each fleet, all behind one hostname and certificate.",
    "A Gateway Load Balancer distributes IP packets to third-party virtual appliances such as firewalls; it does not route HTTP requests by path."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-002", day:5, dom:4, tags:["Load balancing","Encryption in transit"],
 q:"A SaaS startup runs two customer-facing web applications, app.example.com and portal.example.net, each behind its own Application Load Balancer. Each domain has its own TLS certificate in AWS Certificate Manager (ACM). To reduce cost, the team wants to serve both domains from a single load balancer while keeping HTTPS for both. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Use one Network Load Balancer with a TCP listener on port 443 and a listener rule that selects the target group by requested hostname.",
    "Use one ALB with an HTTPS listener holding both ACM certificates and host-based rules that forward each domain to its target group.",
    "Use one ALB with two HTTPS listeners on port 443, each holding one of the certificates and forwarding to its own default target group.",
    "Keep both ALBs and create Route 53 alias records so that each domain name resolves to its own load balancer."],
 a:[1],
 x:["A TCP listener passes encrypted bytes through without reading them, so the NLB cannot see the hostname; NLB listener rules can match only the client's IP address type, not hostnames.",
    "An ALB listener can hold multiple certificates and uses SNI to present the right one; host-header rules then send each domain to its own target group, so one ALB replaces two.",
    "A load balancer cannot have two listeners on the same port; multiple certificates are attached to one listener and selected with SNI.",
    "This works, but it keeps two load balancers running, which does not meet the goal of reducing cost by consolidating onto one."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-003", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Load balancing","Lambda"],
 q:"A media company serves its website from EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer. It wants a new, rarely used /preview/* endpoint to be handled by an AWS Lambda function instead of the instances. Clients must keep using only the ALB's hostname, and no extra load balancer should be added. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Enable a Lambda function URL and add an ALB rule that redirects /preview/* requests to the function URL.",
    "Create a Network Load Balancer with the function as its target and forward /preview/* from the ALB to it.",
    "Package the function code into the web server AMI and add a /preview/* rule that forwards to the existing target group.",
    "Register the function in a Lambda-type target group and add a /preview/* path rule to the existing ALB listener."],
 a:[3],
 x:["A redirect sends the client to the function URL's own hostname, so clients would no longer use only the ALB's hostname.",
    "An NLB target group takes instances, IP addresses or an ALB as targets, not Lambda functions, and the requirement says no extra load balancer should be added.",
    "This runs the code on the instances rather than in Lambda, which defeats the goal of moving a rarely used endpoint to a serverless function.",
    "ALBs support Lambda functions as targets: the ALB invokes the function with the HTTP request as the event, so a path rule can send /preview/* to it behind the same hostname."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-004", day:5, dom:1, tags:["Load balancing","IAM Identity Center & federation"],
 q:"A company hosts an internal expense application on EC2 instances behind an internet-facing Application Load Balancer so that remote employees can reach it without a VPN. The application has no login of its own. Security requires that every request be authenticated against the company's OpenID Connect (OIDC) identity provider before it reaches the instances, without changing the application code. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add an authenticate-oidc action for the company's IdP to the HTTPS listener rule, ahead of the forward action.",
    "Associate an AWS WAF web ACL with the ALB that blocks every request that does not carry an Authorization header.",
    "Enable sticky sessions on the target group so that each user's session stays bound to one authenticated instance.",
    "Allow inbound traffic to the instances only from the ALB's security group so that users cannot bypass the ALB."],
 a:[0],
 x:["ALB listener rules can authenticate users with any OIDC-compliant identity provider, or with Amazon Cognito, before forwarding, so unauthenticated requests never reach the instances and the code is unchanged.",
    "A WAF rule can match on whether a header is present, but such a rule does not verify the user's identity with the IdP, so a forged header would pass.",
    "Stickiness keeps a user's requests on the same target; it does not authenticate anyone.",
    "This prevents bypassing the ALB but authenticates no one, so any internet user could still reach the application through the ALB."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-005", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Load balancing"],
 q:"A financial services company exposes a TCP-based pricing feed on port 9000 to external partners. Partner firewalls allow outbound connections only to a short list of fixed IP addresses, and the feed must stay available if one Availability Zone fails. The service runs in an Auto Scaling group across two Availability Zones. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Place an Application Load Balancer in front of the group and attach an Elastic IP address to each of its subnets.",
    "Place a Network Load Balancer in front of the group and assign an Elastic IP address to it in each Availability Zone.",
    "Run a single EC2 proxy instance with an Elastic IP address that forwards connections to the Auto Scaling group.",
    "Create a Route 53 multivalue answer record that returns the public IP addresses of all healthy instances in the group."],
 a:[1],
 x:["ALB listeners accept only HTTP and HTTPS, not a raw TCP feed, and an ALB's public addresses are service-managed ones, not Elastic IP addresses you attach per subnet; the documented route to a static IP per AZ is a Network Load Balancer.",
    "An internet-facing NLB has one static IP per Availability Zone and can use your own Elastic IPs; it load balances TCP and keeps serving from the other AZ if one fails.",
    "A single proxy instance is a single point of failure and would not survive the loss of its Availability Zone.",
    "Instance IPs change whenever the Auto Scaling group replaces or adds instances, so partners could not keep a fixed allowlist."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-006", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Load balancing"],
 q:"A gaming company runs multiplayer game servers that talk to players over a custom UDP protocol. The load balancer must handle millions of packets per second with very low latency, and the game servers must see each player's original source IP address for anti-cheat checks. Which load balancer should a solutions architect choose?",
 o:["An Application Load Balancer with WebSocket support, forwarding to an instance target group.",
    "A Gateway Load Balancer that forwards traffic to the game servers using the GENEVE protocol.",
    "An Application Load Balancer with sticky sessions so each player stays on one game server.",
    "A Network Load Balancer with a UDP listener, forwarding to an instance target group."],
 a:[3],
 x:["ALBs handle HTTP, HTTPS, WebSockets, and gRPC over TCP; they cannot listen for a custom UDP protocol.",
    "A Gateway Load Balancer is built to pass traffic through third-party virtual appliances such as firewalls, not to front application servers for players.",
    "Stickiness does not change protocol support: ALBs cannot accept UDP traffic, and they replace the client source IP with their own.",
    "NLBs operate at layer 4, support UDP listeners, scale to millions of requests per second with very low latency, and preserve the client source IP for instance targets."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-007", day:5, dom:1, tags:["Encryption in transit","Load balancing"],
 q:"A payments company must keep card data encrypted all the way from the client to its EC2 instances. Compliance rules state that TLS may be decrypted only on the instances, which hold the private keys; the load balancer must never see plaintext. The service uses HTTPS on port 443 and must scale across many instances. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["An Application Load Balancer with an HTTPS listener using an ACM certificate and an HTTPS target group.",
    "A Network Load Balancer with a TCP listener on port 443 that forwards to the instances on port 443.",
    "A Network Load Balancer with a TLS listener using an ACM certificate and a TLS target group.",
    "An Application Load Balancer with an HTTP listener on port 443 that passes the encrypted stream through."],
 a:[1],
 x:["The ALB terminates TLS and re-encrypts to the targets, so traffic is decrypted on the load balancer, which the rules forbid.",
    "A TCP listener passes the encrypted bytes through untouched (TLS passthrough), so only the instances, which hold the private keys, decrypt the traffic.",
    "A TLS listener terminates TLS on the NLB; even with re-encryption to the targets, the load balancer sees plaintext.",
    "ALB listeners are HTTP or HTTPS only: an HTTP listener handles plaintext HTTP and an HTTPS listener terminates TLS on the load balancer. The ALB guide itself points to a TCP listener on a Network Load Balancer for passing encrypted traffic through."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-008", day:5, dom:1, tags:["Load balancing"],
 q:"A company must send all traffic entering its application VPCs through a fleet of third-party next-generation firewall appliances from AWS Marketplace for deep packet inspection. The appliance fleet must scale out, survive the failure of individual appliances, and be transparent to the source and destination of the traffic. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Put the appliances behind a Gateway Load Balancer and route VPC traffic through Gateway Load Balancer endpoints.",
    "Put the appliances behind a Network Load Balancer and point each VPC route table at the NLB's private IP addresses.",
    "Attach an AWS WAF web ACL with Marketplace rule groups to each Application Load Balancer in the VPCs.",
    "Run the appliances as NAT instances in each public subnet and fail over between them with a script."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Gateway Load Balancer is built for third-party virtual appliances: it sits transparently in the traffic path, health-checks and scales the appliance fleet, and receives traffic through endpoints used as route table targets.",
    "An NLB load balances connections addressed to its own listeners; it is not designed to sit transparently in the routing path for all traffic, which is what Gateway Load Balancer does.",
    "AWS WAF inspects only HTTP/HTTPS requests at supported resources; it cannot run third-party firewall appliances or inspect all traffic entering the VPC.",
    "NAT instances with script-based failover are fragile, do not load balance across the fleet, and add significant operational overhead."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-009", day:5, dom:2, tags:["Load balancing"],
 q:"A web application runs on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer. When an instance loses its database connection, the web server still returns HTTP 200 for the home page '/', which is the health check path, so the ALB keeps sending users to the broken instance. How should a solutions architect make the ALB stop routing to these instances?",
 o:["Shorten the target group's health check interval while keeping '/' as the health check path.",
    "Enable sticky sessions so users stay on instances that are already serving pages correctly.",
    "Enable detailed monitoring on the instances so that failures are reported every minute instead of every five minutes.",
    "Point the health check at a /health page that tests the database connection and fails when it is lost."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Checking more often does not help if the check itself keeps passing; '/' still returns 200 while the database is unreachable.",
    "Stickiness pins users to instances but does not detect broken ones; users pinned to a broken instance would keep failing.",
    "Detailed monitoring changes how often EC2 sends metrics to CloudWatch; the ALB does not use these metrics to decide where to route.",
    "Target group health checks decide where the ALB sends traffic. A deep health check that tests the database fails on broken instances, so the ALB marks them unhealthy and stops routing to them."],
 ref:["d5c3"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-010", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Load balancing"],
 q:"A Network Load Balancer spans two Availability Zones. Because of a capacity shortage, AZ A has 2 registered targets and AZ B has 6. Monitoring shows the two targets in AZ A running near 100% CPU while the targets in AZ B are mostly idle. Which change spreads traffic evenly across all eight targets?",
 o:["Turn on sticky sessions for the NLB's target group.",
    "Increase the target group's deregistration delay.",
    "Turn on cross-zone load balancing for the NLB.",
    "Switch the target group's health checks from TCP to HTTP."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Stickiness keeps a client on the same target; it does nothing to rebalance traffic between Availability Zones and can make the imbalance worse.",
    "Deregistration delay controls how long in-flight connections drain when a target is removed; it does not affect how new traffic is distributed.",
    "Cross-zone load balancing is off by default on NLBs, so each AZ's node splits its half of the traffic among only its local targets. Turning it on spreads traffic across all targets (inter-AZ data transfer charges apply on an NLB).",
    "The health check protocol decides how health is tested, not how traffic is spread; busy but responsive targets would still pass."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-011", day:5, dom:2, tags:["Load balancing"],
 q:"A retailer moved a legacy web application behind an Application Load Balancer with four instances. The application keeps each user's shopping cart in the web server's memory, and users now report carts randomly emptying. The team will refactor the application next quarter but needs a fix today without changing any code. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Move the cart data into an Amazon ElastiCache cluster and read it on every request.",
    "Enable load balancer–generated cookie stickiness on the ALB's target group.",
    "Enable cross-zone load balancing so that every instance receives an equal share of requests.",
    "Raise the target group's deregistration delay to its maximum value."],
 a:[1],
 x:["An external session store is the right long-term design, but it requires code changes, which the team cannot make today.",
    "Duration-based stickiness makes the ALB set a cookie that sends a user's requests to the same instance, so in-memory carts stay available without code changes.",
    "Cross-zone load balancing is already on by default for an ALB; an even spread is the cause of the problem, because each request may land on an instance that does not hold the cart.",
    "Deregistration delay only affects draining when a target is removed; it does not keep a user's requests on the same instance."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-012", day:5, dom:2, tags:["Load balancing","Auto Scaling"],
 q:"A reporting application behind an Application Load Balancer streams large CSV exports that can take up to 2 minutes to download. Whenever the Auto Scaling group scales in, some users' downloads fail partway through. The target group's deregistration delay is set to 10 seconds. Which change prevents these failures?",
 o:["Enable sticky sessions so users finish their downloads on the same instance.",
    "Reduce the Auto Scaling group's default cooldown period.",
    "Raise the unhealthy threshold of the target group's health check.",
    "Raise the deregistration delay above the longest download time."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Stickiness controls which instance gets a user's next request; it does not stop the Auto Scaling group from terminating that instance mid-download.",
    "Cooldown controls how soon another scaling activity can start; it does not protect in-flight requests on instances being terminated.",
    "The instances being removed are healthy; health check thresholds play no part in scale-in, so this changes nothing.",
    "Deregistration delay keeps in-flight requests running after the ALB stops sending new ones to a target, and Auto Scaling waits for draining before terminating, so a value above 2 minutes lets downloads finish."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-013", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Auto Scaling","EC2 user data & AMIs"],
 q:"An operations team must roll out a patched AMI to an Auto Scaling group that still uses a launch configuration. They want versioned configurations they can roll back to, support for mixing instance types and purchase options, and a controlled replacement of the running instances. What should they do?",
 o:["Edit the existing launch configuration to reference the new AMI ID, then reboot every instance in the group.",
    "Create a launch template that uses the new AMI, update the group to use it, and start an instance refresh.",
    "Set the group's desired capacity to zero and then back to its original value after registering the new AMI.",
    "Create a new launch configuration for each AMI and use scheduled actions to swap between them."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Launch configurations cannot be edited after creation, and rebooting an instance does not change the AMI it was launched from.",
    "Launch templates support versions (easy rollback), mixed instance types, and On-Demand/Spot mixes; an instance refresh replaces instances in batches while keeping a minimum healthy percentage.",
    "Dropping to zero causes a full outage, and the AMI is set in the launch template or configuration, not registered on the group.",
    "Launch configurations have no versions, no mixed instances policies, and no instance types released since 2023; accounts created since October 2024 cannot create them at all."],
 ref:[], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-014", day:5, dom:2, tags:["Load balancing","High availability design"],
 q:"A company's Auto Scaling group originally used two Availability Zones behind an internet-facing Application Load Balancer. To improve resilience, the team added a subnet in a third AZ to the Auto Scaling group. The new instances in that AZ launch, register with the target group, and run normally, but they never receive requests, and the target group shows them as unused. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Turn on cross-zone load balancing in the target group attributes so that traffic reaches every AZ.",
    "Increase the target group's deregistration delay so that the new instances finish registering.",
    "Enable the third Availability Zone on the ALB by adding a public subnet from that AZ to it.",
    "Create a second ALB in the third AZ and split traffic between both ALBs with Route 53 weights."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Cross-zone load balancing spreads traffic across targets in the AZs that are enabled for the load balancer; it cannot reach targets in an AZ that the ALB is not enabled in.",
    "Deregistration delay controls how long in-flight requests drain when targets leave; it does not affect routing to newly registered targets.",
    "An ALB routes only to targets in Availability Zones that are enabled for it; adding a subnet from the third AZ enables that AZ, so its targets start receiving traffic.",
    "A second load balancer adds cost and complexity, and an ALB needs subnets in at least two AZs anyway; enabling the AZ on the existing ALB is the simple fix."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-015", day:5, dom:2, tags:["Auto Scaling"],
 q:"An Auto Scaling group with a desired capacity of 6 spans two Availability Zones. During an outage in AZ A, the group launched replacements in AZ B, so all 6 instances now run in AZ B. AZ A has now recovered. What will Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling do by default?",
 o:["Keep all 6 instances in AZ B until an administrator rebalances the group across both zones manually.",
    "Launch instances in AZ A first, then terminate instances in AZ B until the zones are even.",
    "Terminate 3 instances in AZ B first, then launch 3 replacements in AZ A.",
    "Live-migrate 3 running instances from AZ B to AZ A without restarting them."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Rebalancing is automatic (the AZRebalance process) unless someone has suspended it; no manual action is needed.",
    "Auto Scaling rebalances across its enabled AZs automatically, launching new instances before terminating old ones so capacity and availability are not reduced.",
    "Terminating first would drop capacity below the desired level; Auto Scaling launches first and may briefly exceed desired capacity while it rebalances.",
    "Rebalancing does not move running instances: Auto Scaling launches new instances in the zones that have the fewest and terminates instances elsewhere."],
 ref:[], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-016", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Auto Scaling"],
 q:"A content site's EC2 instances run behind an Application Load Balancer in an Auto Scaling group. Load testing shows each instance performs well up to about 1,000 requests per minute, but CPU stays low even when instances are overloaded because the work is I/O-bound. The team wants the group to add and remove instances automatically to hold that per-instance load. Which scaling policy should they use?",
 o:["A target tracking policy on average CPU utilization with a target value of 40%.",
    "A simple scaling policy triggered by an alarm on the load balancer's total RequestCount.",
    "Scheduled actions that set desired capacity each hour based on last week's request totals.",
    "A target tracking policy on ALBRequestCountPerTarget with a target value of 1,000."],
 a:[3],
 x:["The work is I/O-bound, so CPU stays low even when instances are overloaded; a CPU target would not add capacity when it is needed.",
    "Total request count ignores how many instances share the load, and simple scaling makes fixed adjustments and waits for a cooldown between them.",
    "Scheduled actions repeat past patterns and cannot react to actual traffic; they also need manual upkeep.",
    "Target tracking on ALBRequestCountPerTarget adds or removes instances to keep average requests per target near 1,000, matching the measured per-instance limit."],
 ref:["d5c2"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-017", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Auto Scaling"],
 q:"An analytics company wants its Auto Scaling group to react in proportion to how far CPU utilization exceeds its threshold: add 1 instance between 60% and 75%, 3 instances between 75% and 90%, and 5 instances above 90%. The group must keep responding to alarm changes even while a previous scaling activity is in progress. Which policy type meets these requirements?",
 o:["A simple scaling policy with one CloudWatch alarm and a cooldown period.",
    "A step scaling policy with step adjustments based on the size of the alarm breach.",
    "A scheduled scaling policy that adds instances at the times CPU is usually high.",
    "A predictive scaling policy that forecasts CPU utilization from the group's historical data."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Simple scaling makes one fixed adjustment and then waits for the cooldown to end before responding to further alarms, so it cannot apply different step sizes.",
    "Step scaling applies different adjustments depending on the size of the alarm breach, and it can start a further scale-out immediately instead of waiting for a cooldown period to end.",
    "Scheduled scaling changes capacity at fixed times and does not react to the actual size of a CPU breach.",
    "Predictive scaling forecasts recurring patterns to launch capacity ahead of time; it does not let you define proportional step adjustments."],
 ref:["d5c2"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-018", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Auto Scaling"],
 q:"A ticketing company knows that tickets for a major festival go on sale at 10:00 on a specific date next month, and traffic will jump to about ten times normal within the first minute. New instances take several minutes to become ready. The site normally runs with target tracking on CPU. What should a solutions architect add so that enough capacity is ready at 10:00?",
 o:["A scheduled action that raises the minimum and desired capacity shortly before 10:00 on that date.",
    "Predictive scaling, so that the group forecasts the spike from its historical load data and launches early.",
    "A lower CPU target value in the existing target tracking policy for that day.",
    "A step scaling policy that adds many instances once CPU exceeds 90%."],
 a:[0],
 x:["The event time is known in advance, so a scheduled action can pre-provision capacity before the sale opens; target tracking stays in place for the rest of the day.",
    "Predictive scaling learns recurring daily and weekly patterns; a one-off sale that has never happened before is not in the history, so it would not be forecast.",
    "A lower target still reacts only after load arrives, and instances take minutes to become ready, so users would hit an under-provisioned site at 10:00.",
    "Step scaling also reacts after the spike starts; with a multi-minute launch time, capacity would arrive too late."],
 ref:["d5c2"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-019", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Auto Scaling"],
 q:"A news website's traffic follows a consistent daily pattern: a sharp ramp every morning, a lunchtime peak, and a quiet night, with a similar shape each week. Instances take several minutes to initialize, so the current target tracking policy adds capacity too late every morning. The team does not want to maintain schedules by hand as the pattern shifts. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create scheduled actions for each weekday and update them each month as traffic changes.",
    "Replace target tracking with step scaling that uses lower CPU thresholds.",
    "Add a predictive scaling policy alongside the existing target tracking policy.",
    "Shorten the default cooldown so that scale-out activities happen more often."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Scheduled actions would launch capacity in time, but they need manual upkeep as the pattern shifts, which the team wants to avoid.",
    "Step scaling is still reactive: it acts only after the metric rises, so instances would still be initializing during the morning ramp.",
    "Predictive scaling analyzes historical load to detect daily and weekly patterns, forecasts the capacity needed and launches it ahead of the ramp; target tracking still handles unexpected changes.",
    "The default cooldown applies to simple scaling; changing it does not make a reactive policy anticipate the morning ramp."],
 ref:["d5c2"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-020", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Auto Scaling"],
 q:"An Auto Scaling group uses a target tracking policy on average CPU. Each new instance spends about 3 minutes loading a cache at 100% CPU before it serves traffic normally. During every scale-out, the group launches far more instances than needed and then scales them back in. The team wants to keep target tracking. What should they change?",
 o:["Set a default instance warm-up for the group that covers the cache-loading time.",
    "Lengthen the health check grace period so that new instances are not checked early.",
    "Lower the target value so that scale-out starts before CPU gets as high.",
    "Turn on ELB health checks so instances are counted only after passing them."],
 a:[0],
 x:["During the warm-up period a new instance's metrics are not included in the group's aggregated metrics, so its startup CPU spike no longer triggers extra scale-out.",
    "The grace period only delays health check–based replacement; it does not stop a new instance's CPU from counting in scaling decisions.",
    "A lower target makes the group scale out earlier and more often, which adds to the overshoot.",
    "ELB health checks decide whether an instance is replaced; they do not exclude new instances from the metric that drives target tracking."],
 ref:["d5c2"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-021", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Auto Scaling"],
 q:"An Auto Scaling group uses a simple scaling policy that adds 2 instances when a CloudWatch CPU alarm enters the ALARM state. After the first scale-out, the alarm stays in ALARM, but no more instances launch for several minutes even though the group is well below its maximum capacity. What explains this behavior?",
 o:["The new instances are still within their health check grace period.",
    "The group has reached its desired capacity, which blocks further scaling.",
    "The target group's deregistration delay must finish before new targets can be registered.",
    "Simple scaling waits for the cooldown period to end before acting on the alarm again."],
 a:[3],
 x:["The grace period delays health checks on new instances; it does not stop a scaling policy from acting on an alarm.",
    "Desired capacity is what scaling policies change; only the maximum capacity caps scale-out, and the group is below it.",
    "Deregistration delay affects only targets being removed; it does not block launching or registering new instances.",
    "After a simple scaling activity, the group waits for the cooldown period before responding to further alarms; step and target tracking policies do not have this limitation."],
 ref:["d5c2"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-022", day:5, dom:2, tags:["Auto Scaling"],
 q:"A machine learning inference service runs in an Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer. Before a new instance can serve requests, a script must download a large model file and register the instance with a third-party license server, which can take up to 10 minutes. The instance must not be put in service or registered with the load balancer until the script succeeds. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Lengthen the health check grace period to 10 minutes so that new instances are not checked too early.",
    "Add a launch lifecycle hook; the script calls <code>complete-lifecycle-action</code> when done.",
    "Run the script from user data and set the group's default cooldown to 10 minutes.",
    "Use a scheduled action that launches instances 10 minutes before they are needed."],
 a:[1],
 x:["The grace period only delays health check–based replacement; the instance still enters InService and is registered with the target group before the script finishes.",
    "A launch lifecycle hook holds the instance in Pending:Wait; it is registered and put in service only after the script signals CONTINUE with complete-lifecycle-action.",
    "User data runs at boot, but the cooldown only delays further scaling activities; it does not keep the instance out of service.",
    "Scheduled actions cannot cover unplanned scale-outs or replacements, and they do not stop an instance from entering service before the script is done."],
 ref:[], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-023", day:5, dom:2, tags:["Auto Scaling"],
 q:"An application writes audit logs to local disk on each instance in an Auto Scaling group, and the team cannot change how the application logs. When the group scales in, the logs on terminated instances are lost. Every log file must be copied to an S3 bucket before an instance is terminated. Which solution meets this requirement?",
 o:["Enable instance scale-in protection on every instance in the group so none are removed early.",
    "Increase the target group's deregistration delay to its maximum value of one hour.",
    "Change the group's termination policy so that the oldest instance is always terminated first.",
    "Add a termination lifecycle hook that holds the instance while a script copies logs to S3."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Scale-in protection stops the group from terminating instances during scale-in, so it could never shrink; it does not copy any logs.",
    "Deregistration delay only lets in-flight requests finish; it runs no script and does not guarantee the logs are copied.",
    "The termination policy only chooses which instance is removed; the chosen instance's logs are still lost.",
    "A termination lifecycle hook holds the instance in Terminating:Wait, giving a script (for example started through EventBridge and Systems Manager) time to copy the logs before termination continues."],
 ref:[], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-024", day:5, dom:2, tags:["Auto Scaling","Load balancing"],
 q:"An Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer uses the default health check settings. Sometimes the application process hangs: the ALB marks the target unhealthy and stops sending it traffic, but the instance keeps running and is never replaced, so the group runs with less capacity. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Turn on Elastic Load Balancing health checks for the Auto Scaling group.",
    "Enable detailed monitoring so that EC2 reports instance metrics every minute.",
    "Create a CloudWatch alarm that reboots an instance when StatusCheckFailed is reported.",
    "Increase the health check grace period for the Auto Scaling group."],
 a:[0],
 x:["By default the group uses only EC2 status checks, which pass while the OS runs. With ELB health checks on, the group also treats targets the ALB reports unhealthy as unhealthy and replaces them.",
    "Detailed monitoring increases metric frequency but does not change how the group decides an instance is unhealthy.",
    "The instance passes its EC2 status checks because only the application has hung, so this alarm would never fire.",
    "A longer grace period only delays health checks further; the group still would not use the ALB's view of target health."],
 ref:["d5c3"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-025", day:5, dom:2, tags:["Auto Scaling","SQS"],
 q:"A media company renders videos on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group that scales on the length of an SQS queue. A render job can take up to 90 minutes. When the queue drains, scale-in sometimes terminates instances that are in the middle of a job, wasting the work, while idle instances keep running. The company wants scale-in to remove only idle instances. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Increase the Auto Scaling group's default cooldown to 90 minutes so that scale-in waits for jobs to end.",
    "Add a termination lifecycle hook that keeps each terminating instance in a wait state until its job ends.",
    "Have each worker enable instance scale-in protection when it starts a job and remove it when the job ends.",
    "Suspend the Terminate process on the Auto Scaling group so that no instance is ever removed by scale-in."],
 a:[2],
 x:["A cooldown only delays scaling activities; when scale-in does happen, the group can still choose a busy instance.",
    "A lifecycle hook delays the termination of the instance the group chose, but the group can still choose a busy instance while idle ones keep running, so scale-in is held up instead of removing idle capacity.",
    "Protected instances are never selected for scale-in, so the group terminates only unprotected, idle instances, and each worker removes the protection as soon as its job finishes.",
    "Suspending Terminate stops all scale-in, so the group never shrinks and the company pays for idle instances."],
 ref:["d5c2"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-026", day:5, dom:2, tags:["DynamoDB","High availability design"],
 q:"A startup is making its web tier stateless and needs a place to keep user sessions. The store must be durable, serverless with no capacity planning, and highly available across Availability Zones, and it must expire sessions automatically 24 hours after their last update at no extra cost. Which solution meets these requirements with the least operational overhead?",
 o:["An Amazon RDS for MySQL Multi-AZ DB instance with a scheduled job that deletes old sessions.",
    "An Amazon DynamoDB table in on-demand capacity mode with Time to Live (TTL) enabled.",
    "An Amazon EFS file system mounted on every instance, with a cron job that removes old session files.",
    "ALB sticky sessions with a cookie duration of 24 hours on the target group."],
 a:[1],
 x:["RDS needs instance sizing and a cleanup job you maintain, so it is neither serverless nor the lowest overhead.",
    "DynamoDB is serverless and replicates across AZs, and on-demand mode needs no capacity planning. TTL deletes items past the expiry time the app sets without consuming write throughput; deletion can lag by a few days, so reads filter on the expiry attribute.",
    "EFS is shared and durable, but session cleanup needs a cron job you must run and maintain, which adds overhead.",
    "Stickiness leaves sessions in instance memory; they are not durable and are lost when the instance is terminated."],
 ref:[], doc:"dynamodb"},

{id:"q05-027", day:5, dom:2, tags:["RDS","High availability design"],
 q:"A company's web tier runs in an Auto Scaling group across two Availability Zones behind an Application Load Balancer, but its Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database is a Single-AZ DB instance. An architecture review flags the database as a single point of failure. The company needs automatic failover with no connection-string changes and no loss of committed transactions. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Create a read replica in the second AZ and promote it manually if the primary fails.",
    "Enable automated backups and restore the latest snapshot in another AZ if the primary fails.",
    "Convert the DB instance to a Multi-AZ deployment with a standby in another AZ.",
    "Launch a second DB instance in the other AZ and register both behind the ALB."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Read replicas use asynchronous replication, so recent transactions can be lost, and promotion is manual and gives the application a different endpoint.",
    "Restoring from a backup takes time, loses changes since the latest restorable point, and creates a new endpoint the application must be pointed to.",
    "Multi-AZ replicates synchronously to a standby in another AZ and fails over automatically; the DNS endpoint stays the same, so the application only needs to reconnect.",
    "ALBs route HTTP traffic and do not replicate data; two independent databases would diverge, and the ALB cannot fail over database connections."],
 ref:["d3c2"], doc:"rds"},

{id:"q05-028", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Auto Scaling","SQS"],
 q:"Video-transcoding workers in an Auto Scaling group pull jobs from an Amazon SQS queue. Each worker runs near 100% CPU whenever it has work, whether the queue holds 10 messages or 10,000, so the current CPU-based policy does not keep up during peaks. The company wants the fleet sized to clear the queue within an acceptable time. Which scaling approach should a solutions architect use?",
 o:["Target tracking on a custom metric: visible messages divided by the number of running instances.",
    "Target tracking on average CPU utilization with the target value lowered to 50%.",
    "A simple scaling policy on the queue's NumberOfMessagesSent metric with a long cooldown.",
    "Increase the queue's visibility timeout so that messages are handed to the workers more slowly."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Backlog per instance (ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible divided by InService instances) shows how much work each worker faces; targeting an acceptable backlog keeps the queue draining in time.",
    "CPU is near 100% whenever there is any work, so it cannot tell 10 messages from 10,000; the fleet would still be sized wrongly.",
    "Messages sent measures the arrival rate, not the backlog waiting to be processed, and simple scaling with a long cooldown reacts slowly.",
    "Visibility timeout controls how long a received message stays hidden; it adds no processing capacity."],
 ref:["d5c2"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-029", day:5, dom:2, tags:["Route 53"],
 q:"A company uses Route 53 failover records in a private hosted zone to send an internal API's traffic to a primary internal Application Load Balancer, with a secondary ALB in another Region. The ALBs have only private IP addresses. The team needs a Route 53 health check that marks the primary unhealthy when its target group has no healthy targets. Which health check configuration meets this requirement?",
 o:["An endpoint health check that targets the primary ALB's private IP addresses on port 443.",
    "A health check that monitors a CloudWatch alarm on the primary target group's HealthyHostCount metric.",
    "An endpoint health check on the private IPs, plus security group rules that allow the Route 53 health checker IP ranges.",
    "A calculated health check that uses the ALB target group's own health checks as its child checks."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Route 53 health checkers run on the internet and cannot reach private IP addresses inside a VPC, so this check would never succeed.",
    "For private resources, Route 53 can base a health check on a CloudWatch alarm; an alarm on HealthyHostCount dropping below 1 marks the primary unhealthy so the failover records answer with the secondary.",
    "Opening security groups does not help: the health checkers have no network path to private IP addresses in the VPC.",
    "Calculated health checks combine other Route 53 health checks; target group health checks are not Route 53 health checks and cannot be children."],
 ref:[], doc:"route53"},

{id:"q05-030", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Load balancing"],
 q:"A mobile gaming company is releasing version 2 of its backend API. Updated mobile clients send the HTTP header X-Client-Version: 2 and use the same hostname and URL paths as older clients. Requests with that header must go to a new fleet of instances, while all other requests stay on the existing fleet. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Add a path-based rule on the ALB listener that forwards /v2/* requests to the new fleet's target group.",
    "Create Route 53 weighted records that send a share of traffic to a new ALB in front of the new fleet.",
    "Add a Network Load Balancer listener rule that reads the header and forwards to the new fleet.",
    "Add an ALB listener rule with an HTTP header condition that forwards to the new fleet's target group."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Updated clients do not change their URL paths, so a path rule would never match their requests.",
    "Weighted DNS splits traffic by percentage regardless of client version, so older clients would also reach version 2.",
    "NLBs operate at layer 4 and cannot inspect HTTP headers; their listener rules can match only the client's IP address type, not request content.",
    "ALB listener rules can match HTTP headers, so requests carrying X-Client-Version: 2 go to the new target group and everything else falls through to the default action."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-031", day:5, dom:1, tags:["Encryption in transit","Load balancing"],
 q:"A government agency's public website runs behind an Application Load Balancer. A new policy requires that every visitor who arrives over HTTP is redirected to HTTPS, that certificates renew automatically, and that no changes are made to the web servers. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Remove the port 80 rule from the ALB's security group so that only HTTPS connections are accepted.",
    "Add an HTTP listener with a redirect action to HTTPS, and use an ACM certificate on the HTTPS listener.",
    "Install certificates on each web server and configure every server to redirect HTTP requests to HTTPS.",
    "Replace the ALB with a Network Load Balancer that has a TLS listener on 443 and no listener on port 80."],
 a:[1],
 x:["Blocking port 80 makes HTTP visitors time out instead of being redirected, which fails the requirement.",
    "ALB listeners support redirect actions, so HTTP requests are redirected to HTTPS at the load balancer; ACM renews the certificates it issues automatically when they use DNS validation, with no server changes.",
    "This changes every web server and leaves certificate renewal as a manual task on each instance.",
    "An NLB listener only forwards connections to target groups and has no HTTP redirect action, and without a port 80 listener HTTP visitors simply fail to connect."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"acm"},

{id:"q05-032", day:5, dom:1, tags:["Security groups & NACLs","Load balancing"],
 q:"A web application's EC2 instances run in private subnets behind an internet-facing Application Load Balancer. The instances must accept HTTP traffic only from the ALB, and the ALB's node IP addresses can change as it scales. Which solution meets these requirements with the least maintenance?",
 o:["In the instances' security group, allow port 80 from the security group attached to the ALB.",
    "In the instances' security group, allow port 80 from the ALB nodes' current private IP addresses.",
    "In the private subnets' network ACL, allow port 80 from the VPC CIDR and deny all other inbound traffic.",
    "In the instances' security group, allow port 80 from <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> because the instances are in private subnets."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Referencing the ALB's security group as the source allows traffic from any ALB node, whatever its IP, and from nothing else, so no rule updates are needed as the ALB scales.",
    "ALB node IPs change as the load balancer scales or is maintained, so the rule would need constant updates and would break.",
    "Allowing the whole VPC CIDR lets any resource in the VPC reach the instances, not only the ALB, and stateless NACL rules add maintenance.",
    "Private subnets block direct internet access, but this rule still lets any resource in the VPC or connected networks reach the instances, which breaks the ALB-only requirement."],
 ref:["d2c1"], doc:"vpc"},

{id:"q05-033", day:5, dom:1, tags:["Load balancing"],
 q:"A security team needs the original client IP address of every request recorded in the application logs of web servers that sit behind an Application Load Balancer. Currently the logs show only the private IP addresses of the ALB nodes. What should a solutions architect do?",
 o:["Configure the web servers to log the X-Forwarded-For request header.",
    "Enable proxy protocol version 2 on the ALB's target group.",
    "Assign Elastic IP addresses to the ALB so that the original client IPs are preserved.",
    "Turn on cross-zone load balancing so that client connections are no longer proxied."],
 a:[0],
 x:["The ALB terminates the client connection and opens its own to the target, adding the client IP in the X-Forwarded-For header, which the web server can log.",
    "Proxy protocol version 2 is how a Network Load Balancer passes the client address to its targets; an ALB adds the X-Forwarded-For header instead.",
    "Whatever public addresses the ALB has, it still opens its own connection to each target, so the servers keep seeing the load balancer's address as the source.",
    "Cross-zone load balancing only controls which AZs' targets receive traffic; the ALB still proxies every connection."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-034", day:5, dom:4, tags:["Auto Scaling","EC2 purchase options"],
 q:"A stateless web tier needs 4 instances at all times and bursts up to 20 instances during unpredictable peaks. The burst capacity can tolerate occasional interruptions, but the baseline cannot. The company wants to minimize compute cost while keeping a single Auto Scaling group. Which configuration meets these requirements?",
 o:["Run all instances as Spot Instances of one instance type to get the largest discount.",
    "Buy Reserved Instances to cover all 20 instances so that every possible peak is discounted.",
    "Use a mixed instances policy: On-Demand base of 4, Spot across several instance types above it.",
    "Create two Auto Scaling groups, one On-Demand and one Spot, each registered behind its own load balancer."],
 a:[2],
 x:["The baseline cannot tolerate interruptions, and a single instance type makes Spot interruptions and capacity shortages more likely.",
    "Paying for 20 reserved instances wastes money during the long periods when only the 4 baseline instances are needed.",
    "A mixed instances policy keeps the baseline On-Demand and fills burst capacity with Spot; several instance types with the price-capacity-optimized strategy lower the chance of interruption.",
    "Two groups and two load balancers add cost and complexity and break the single-group requirement."],
 ref:["d1c7"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-035", day:5, dom:2, tags:["Load balancing"],
 q:"A fintech company wants to release a new version of its web application by sending 10% of requests to a new (green) fleet and 90% to the current (blue) fleet, both behind the same Application Load Balancer. If errors rise, the team must be able to send all traffic back to blue immediately, without waiting for DNS caches to expire. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create Route 53 weighted records that send 10% of lookups to a separate ALB for the green fleet.",
    "Enable slow start mode on the green target group so it receives only 10% of requests.",
    "Enable sticky sessions on the blue target group so that 90% of users remain on the current version.",
    "Use a listener rule that forwards to the blue and green target groups with weights of 90 and 10."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Weighted DNS works for canaries, but rolling back depends on resolvers and clients honoring the TTL, so traffic does not move back immediately; it also needs a second ALB.",
    "Slow start ramps a new target's share up gradually over a set period and then gives it a normal share; it cannot hold a fixed 10% split.",
    "Stickiness keeps returning users on the same target but does not set what share of traffic each version receives.",
    "ALB forward actions support weighted target groups, so the split lives on the listener; setting the weights to 100 and 0 moves traffic back to blue at once."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-036", day:5, dom:2, tags:["Auto Scaling"],
 q:"After turning on ELB health checks for an Auto Scaling group, a team sees new instances terminated and replaced in a loop shortly after launch. The application takes about 4 minutes to start, and the health check grace period is set to 60 seconds. What should the team do to stop the loop while keeping ELB health checks?",
 o:["Set the health check grace period longer than the application's startup time.",
    "Increase the target group's deregistration delay to 4 minutes to match startup.",
    "Lower the unhealthy threshold count in the target group's health check.",
    "Shorten the default cooldown so that replacement instances launch more quickly."],
 a:[0],
 x:["The grace period tells the group to wait before acting on health check results for a new instance; once it covers the 4-minute startup, instances are not replaced before the application is ready.",
    "Deregistration delay applies when targets are removed; it does not stop the group from treating starting instances as unhealthy.",
    "A lower unhealthy threshold marks targets unhealthy even sooner, which makes the loop worse.",
    "Faster replacements only speed up the loop; new instances would still be marked unhealthy before they finish starting."],
 ref:["d5c3"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-037", day:5, dom:2, tags:["Auto Scaling"],
 q:"An engineer needs to troubleshoot an EC2 instance that belongs to an Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer. While the engineer works on it, the instance must stop receiving traffic, must not be terminated because of health checks, and must be able to return to the group afterward. What should the engineer do?",
 o:["Enable instance scale-in protection on the instance.",
    "Suspend the Launch process for the Auto Scaling group.",
    "Stop the instance from the EC2 console until the work is done.",
    "Put the instance into the Standby state in the group."],
 a:[3],
 x:["Scale-in protection only stops the group from choosing the instance during scale-in; it does not protect the instance from being replaced when it fails health checks.",
    "Suspending Launch stops new instances from starting; the instance still receives traffic and can be marked unhealthy and terminated.",
    "Auto Scaling treats any instance that is not in the running state as unhealthy, so a stopped instance is terminated and replaced.",
    "Standby keeps the instance in the group but deregisters it from the load balancer and stops health checks against it; it can be moved back to InService when the work is done."],
 ref:[], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-038", day:5, dom:2, tags:["Auto Scaling","Load balancing"],
 q:"A team creates an Application Load Balancer, a target group, and an Auto Scaling group from a launch template. The instances launch and pass EC2 status checks, but browsing to the ALB's DNS name returns HTTP 503, and the target group shows no registered targets. What is the most likely fix?",
 o:["Attach the target group to the Auto Scaling group so instances register automatically.",
    "Enable cross-zone load balancing on the ALB so that it can see the targets in every zone.",
    "Add an Elastic IP address to each instance so that the ALB can reach the instances.",
    "Turn on sticky sessions so that the ALB keeps sending requests to the same instance."],
 a:[0],
 x:["A 503 with an empty target group means no targets are registered; attaching the target group to the Auto Scaling group registers current and future instances automatically.",
    "Cross-zone load balancing affects how traffic is spread among registered targets; with no targets registered, there is nowhere to send traffic.",
    "The ALB reaches targets on their private IPs inside the VPC, so Elastic IPs are not needed and would not register anything.",
    "Stickiness needs registered, healthy targets to work; it does not register instances."],
 ref:[], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-039", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Load balancing"],
 q:"A B2B company's web application uses an Application Load Balancer with path-based routing to several target groups. A large customer now requires fixed IP addresses for its firewall allowlist. The company wants to keep the existing path-based routing and avoid rewriting listener rules. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Attach an Elastic IP address to each subnet that the Application Load Balancer uses.",
    "Replace the ALB with a Network Load Balancer and recreate the path rules on its listener.",
    "Put a Network Load Balancer with Elastic IPs in front, using the ALB as its target.",
    "Replace the ALB with a Gateway Load Balancer, which provides one static IP per zone."],
 a:[2],
 x:["An ALB's public addresses are assigned and managed by the service (an IPAM pool can only confine them to an address range you bring); they are not Elastic IP addresses you attach to its subnets. AWS's documented way to give an ALB a static IP per AZ is a Network Load Balancer in front of it.",
    "NLBs work at layer 4 and do not support path-based rules, so the routing would be lost.",
    "An NLB can use an ALB as its target (an ALB-type target group): the NLB provides static Elastic IPs per AZ and the ALB keeps its path rules. AWS Global Accelerator in front of the ALB is another way to get static IPs.",
    "Gateway Load Balancers pass traffic through virtual appliances; they do not serve client HTTP traffic or do path-based routing."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-040", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Load balancing"],
 q:"An e-commerce site's Application Load Balancer sends a full share of requests to each new instance as soon as it passes its health check. Each new instance's in-memory cache fills only as it serves requests, so response times on new instances spike badly for the first few minutes. What should a solutions architect configure so that new targets receive traffic gradually?",
 o:["Enable slow start mode on the target group with a duration that covers the cache warm-up.",
    "Increase the target group's deregistration delay to the cache warm-up time.",
    "Enable sticky sessions so that existing users stay on the older instances.",
    "Increase the healthy threshold so that new targets take longer to pass their health checks."],
 a:[0],
 x:["Slow start linearly increases the share of requests a newly registered target receives over the set duration, so its cache warms up before it takes a full share.",
    "Deregistration delay only affects targets being removed; it does nothing for new targets.",
    "Stickiness keeps existing users where they are, but new users would still be sent to cold instances at a full share.",
    "The cache fills only as the instance serves requests, so delaying the first request does not warm it; the instance would still get a full share at once."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-041", day:5, dom:3, tags:["RDS"],
 q:"An application uses an Amazon RDS for MySQL Multi-AZ DB instance deployment. Heavy reporting queries are slowing down the primary, and a developer suggests pointing the reports at the Multi-AZ standby because it is otherwise idle. What should a solutions architect recommend?",
 o:["Point the reporting queries at the standby's endpoint, as the developer suggests.",
    "Disable Multi-AZ and use the freed capacity to run the reports on the primary.",
    "Add an Application Load Balancer that splits queries between the primary and the standby.",
    "Create a read replica and send the reporting queries to the replica's endpoint."],
 a:[3],
 x:["In a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment, the standby exists only for failover and cannot serve read traffic.",
    "Turning off Multi-AZ removes automatic failover and adds no read capacity to the primary.",
    "ALBs balance HTTP traffic, not database connections, and the standby cannot accept queries anyway.",
    "A read replica serves read-only queries from asynchronously replicated data, offloading reports from the primary while Multi-AZ keeps providing availability."],
 ref:["d3c2"], doc:"rds"},

{id:"q05-042", day:5, dom:4, tags:["Auto Scaling"],
 q:"During a cost review, an engineer wants to permanently reduce an Auto Scaling group from 4 running instances to 3. The group is set to minimum 4, desired 4, and maximum 8. The engineer terminated one instance in the EC2 console, but a new one launched a few minutes later. What should the engineer do?",
 o:["Enable scale-in protection on the remaining instances, then terminate the extra instance again.",
    "Lower the maximum capacity of the group to 3 and leave the other settings unchanged.",
    "Lower the group's minimum and desired capacity to 3.",
    "Suspend the HealthCheck process so the group stops replacing the terminated instance."],
 a:[2],
 x:["Scale-in protection does not change the desired capacity, so the group would still launch a replacement to get back to 4.",
    "The maximum cannot be lower than the minimum, so a maximum of 3 is rejected while the minimum is still 4; the minimum has to come down before the group can shrink.",
    "An Auto Scaling group always works to maintain its desired capacity; lowering desired, and the minimum that would otherwise block it, to 3 makes the reduction permanent.",
    "Suspending processes is meant for short troubleshooting; the desired capacity stays at 4, so the group returns to 4 instances when processes resume."],
 ref:[], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-043", day:5, dom:1, tags:["Load balancing","VPC & subnets"],
 q:"A three-tier application has a web tier behind an internet-facing Application Load Balancer. The application tier, which the web tier calls over HTTP, also needs load balancing across Availability Zones but must not be reachable from the internet. Which solution meets these requirements?",
 o:["Create an internet-facing ALB for the application tier and restrict it with a network ACL.",
    "Add path rules to the existing internet-facing ALB that forward to the application tier.",
    "Create an internal ALB in private subnets in two AZs for the application tier.",
    "Have the web tier call application instances directly using a list of their private IPs."],
 a:[2],
 x:["An internet-facing ALB has public IP addresses; relying on NACL rules to hide it adds risk and maintenance compared with an internal load balancer.",
    "This would expose the application tier through the public load balancer, which breaks the requirement.",
    "An internal ALB has only private IP addresses, so it load balances across AZs while staying unreachable from the internet.",
    "A static IP list breaks as Auto Scaling replaces instances and gives no health-checked load balancing."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-044", day:5, dom:2, tags:["High availability design","Auto Scaling"],
 q:"A startup runs its web application on a single EC2 instance with an Elastic IP address in one public subnet. The founders want the application to keep serving users if an instance or an entire Availability Zone fails, and failed instances must be replaced automatically. Which combination of steps should a solutions architect take? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Create an Auto Scaling group from a launch template across two AZs, with a minimum of 2.",
    "Move the application to a larger instance type with enhanced networking enabled.",
    "Launch the instances in a cluster placement group to keep them close together.",
    "Put an Application Load Balancer across both AZs in front of the group and point DNS at it.",
    "Write a script that moves the Elastic IP address to a standby instance when the primary fails."],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["At least two instances spread across two AZs survive an instance or AZ failure, and the group replaces failed instances automatically.",
    "A bigger instance is still a single instance in one AZ, so it remains a single point of failure.",
    "Cluster placement groups keep instances in a single AZ for low latency, which reduces availability instead of improving it.",
    "An ALB spanning both AZs gives users one stable DNS name, health-checks the instances, and routes only to healthy ones in either AZ.",
    "A custom failover script is fragile, slow, and needs maintenance; a load balancer with Auto Scaling handles this as a managed service."],
 ref:[], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-045", day:5, dom:2, tags:["High availability design","Caching"],
 q:"A photo-sharing application keeps user sessions in each web server's memory and saves uploaded photos to each server's EBS volume. The company wants to run the web tier in an Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer so that any instance can serve any request and instances can be terminated at any time without losing data. Which combination of changes is required? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Enable sticky sessions on the target group with a long cookie duration.",
    "Move session data to an external store such as Amazon ElastiCache.",
    "Attach one EBS volume to all instances in every AZ with EBS Multi-Attach.",
    "Take hourly EBS snapshots of each web server's volume and keep them for 7 days.",
    "Store uploaded photos in Amazon S3 instead of on the instances' EBS volumes."],
 a:[1,4],
 x:["Stickiness keeps state on individual instances, so sessions are still lost when an instance is terminated.",
    "Keeping sessions in a shared store such as ElastiCache (or DynamoDB) makes the web servers stateless, so any instance can handle any request.",
    "Multi-Attach works only for io1/io2 volumes within a single AZ and needs a cluster-aware file system; it cannot be shared across AZs.",
    "Snapshots run on a schedule, so photos uploaded since the last one are lost, and other instances still cannot see them.",
    "S3 gives every instance the same durable photo storage, so terminating an instance loses nothing."],
 ref:[], doc:"elasticache"},

{id:"q05-046", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Load balancing"],
 q:"A solutions architect is choosing between an Application Load Balancer and a Network Load Balancer for a new workload. Which capabilities does a Network Load Balancer provide that an Application Load Balancer does not? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Routing requests to different target groups based on the request URL path.",
    "A static IP address per Availability Zone, optionally an Elastic IP.",
    "Invoking AWS Lambda functions as targets of the load balancer.",
    "Listeners for UDP traffic as well as TCP and TLS traffic.",
    "Direct integration with AWS WAF web ACLs."],
 a:[1,3],
 x:["Path-based routing is a layer 7 ALB feature; NLBs cannot inspect HTTP requests.",
    "NLBs expose one static IP per AZ, and internet-facing NLBs can use your own Elastic IPs; an ALB's addresses are assigned and managed by the service.",
    "Lambda targets are supported by ALBs, not NLBs.",
    "NLBs support layer 4 listeners: TCP, UDP, TCP_UDP, TLS, and (since late 2025) QUIC; ALB listeners are only HTTP and HTTPS.",
    "An AWS WAF web ACL (a protection pack in the current docs) protects ALBs and resources such as CloudFront distributions and API Gateway APIs; Network Load Balancers are not on the list of resources it can protect."],
 ref:["d5c1"], doc:"elb"},

{id:"q05-047", day:5, dom:2, tags:["High availability design","Internet & NAT gateways"],
 q:"An order-processing application runs in an Auto Scaling group across three Availability Zones behind an Application Load Balancer. The instances in private subnets call an external payment API through a single NAT gateway in one AZ, and they store orders in a Single-AZ Amazon RDS for MySQL DB instance. Which combination of changes removes the remaining single points of failure? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Add a second internet gateway to the VPC and route half of the private subnets through it.",
    "Deploy one NAT gateway per AZ and route each private subnet to the one in its own AZ.",
    "Convert the RDS DB instance to a Multi-AZ deployment.",
    "Add a read replica of the RDS DB instance in the same Availability Zone.",
    "Replace the NAT gateway with a larger NAT instance that has an Elastic IP."],
 a:[1,2],
 x:["A VPC can have only one internet gateway, and it is already highly available; the NAT gateway and database are the single points of failure.",
    "A standard (zonal) NAT gateway lives in one AZ; one per AZ with zone-local routes keeps outbound access working if any single AZ fails. A regional NAT gateway, which spans the AZs automatically, is a newer way to get the same result.",
    "Multi-AZ adds a synchronous standby in another AZ with automatic failover, so the database survives an instance or AZ failure.",
    "A replica in the same AZ fails along with that AZ, uses asynchronous replication, and needs manual promotion, so it does not remove the single point of failure.",
    "A single NAT instance is still in one AZ and adds patching and failover work, so it is no more available than one NAT gateway."],
 ref:["d3c2","d2b1"], doc:"rds"},

{id:"q05-048", day:5, dom:3, tags:["Auto Scaling","EC2 user data & AMIs"],
 q:"A video platform's Auto Scaling group needs about 12 minutes to bring a new instance into service because the user data script downloads and compiles dependencies at every boot. Traffic spikes are sudden and unpredictable, so capacity arrives too late. Which combination of actions will most reduce the time it takes to add serving capacity? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Build a custom AMI with the dependencies preinstalled and use it in the launch template.",
    "Increase the health check grace period so that new instances are not checked too early.",
    "Add a warm pool of pre-initialized instances kept in the Stopped state.",
    "Switch to a simple scaling policy with a longer cooldown period.",
    "Add a scheduled action that raises capacity every morning before business hours begin."],
 a:[0,2],
 x:["Baking dependencies into a custom AMI removes the download-and-compile step from boot, so new instances start serving much sooner.",
    "A longer grace period only delays health checks; it does not make instances ready any faster.",
    "A warm pool keeps already-initialized instances stopped, so you pay mainly for their EBS storage, and they can be started and put in service much faster than new launches.",
    "Simple scaling with a longer cooldown reacts more slowly, which makes the late-capacity problem worse.",
    "The spikes are unpredictable, so a fixed morning schedule would not line up with them."],
 ref:[], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-049", day:5, dom:4, tags:["Auto Scaling","EC2 purchase options"],
 q:"A company's internal HR portal runs on 8 On-Demand instances in an Auto Scaling group across two Availability Zones, 24 hours a day. It is busy only from 07:00 to 19:00 on weekdays, but it must stay available in two AZs at all times and cannot tolerate interruptions. The portal will run for at least three more years. Which combination of actions is most cost-effective? (Choose two.)",
 o:["Add scheduled actions that drop to 2 instances after hours and restore 8 before 07:00.",
    "Scale the group to zero every evening and back up to 8 instances each morning before 07:00.",
    "Move all instances into a single Availability Zone outside business hours.",
    "Purchase a Compute Savings Plan that covers the always-on baseline usage.",
    "Replace every instance with Spot Instances, including the 2-instance overnight baseline."],
 a:[0,3],
 x:["Scheduled actions match capacity to the known business-hours pattern while keeping one instance in each AZ overnight, so the portal stays available in two AZs.",
    "Scaling to zero makes the portal unavailable overnight, which breaks the always-available requirement.",
    "Running in one AZ breaks the requirement to stay available in two AZs at all times.",
    "The 2-instance baseline runs every hour for years, which is exactly the steady usage a Savings Plan discounts compared with On-Demand.",
    "The portal cannot tolerate interruptions, and Spot Instances can be reclaimed with a two-minute notice."],
 ref:["d5c2","d1c7"], doc:"autoscaling"},

{id:"q05-050", day:5, dom:2, tags:["Route 53","High availability design"],
 q:"A company hosts www.example.com on an Application Load Balancer in eu-west-1. If the application becomes unavailable, visitors must automatically see a static maintenance page served from an Amazon S3 bucket configured for website hosting. Which combination of Route 53 configurations meets this requirement? (Choose two.)",
 o:["A latency-based alias record for www.example.com that points to the S3 bucket's website endpoint.",
    "A primary failover alias record that points to the ALB with Evaluate Target Health set to Yes.",
    "A simple routing record that lists both the ALB and the S3 website endpoint as its values.",
    "A weighted record that sends 50% of traffic to the S3 website endpoint.",
    "A secondary failover alias record that points to the S3 website endpoint for the bucket."],
 a:[1,4],
 x:["Latency routing picks the lowest-latency record for each user, not a backup when the primary fails; users could get the maintenance page while the app is healthy.",
    "The primary failover record answers with the ALB while it is healthy; with Evaluate Target Health, Route 53 treats it as unhealthy when the ALB has no healthy targets.",
    "A simple alias record can point to only one AWS resource, and simple routing does no health checking, so it cannot answer with the ALB normally and switch to the bucket only when the ALB fails.",
    "A 50% weight sends half of all visitors to the maintenance page even while the application is healthy.",
    "The secondary failover record is returned only when the primary is unhealthy, sending visitors to the S3 maintenance page; the bucket must be named www.example.com for the alias to work."],
 ref:[], doc:"route53"}
);
